.SH "NAME"
nvme-keysd \- NVMe/TCP TLS key provisioning daemon
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fInvme\-keysd\fR [\-\-config=<FILE>] [\-\-fabrics\-config=<FILE>]
                [\-\-creds\-dir=<DIR>] [\-\-kmip\-test\-addr=<PATH>]
                [\-\-should\-start] [\-\-debug] [\-\-help]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
nvme\-keysd puts the TLS pre\-shared keys (PSKs) used by NVMe/TCP into the kernel\(cqs \&.nvme keyring, before a connection needs them\&. The kernel then finds the key there when \fInvme connect\fR or nvme\-discoverd connects with TLS\&.
.sp
Which key belongs to which host and subsystem is configured in the shared NVMe\-oF fabrics configuration, see nvme\-fabrics\&.conf(5)\&.
.sp
This is a technology preview\&.
.SH "KEYS"
.sp
nvme\-keysd imports every entry of the fabrics configuration that has \fItls\-key\-source = systemd\-creds\fR or \fItls\-key\-source = kmip\fR\&. With \fIsystemd\-creds\fR, \fItls\-key\fR is the name of a systemd credential\&. The credential holds the PSK in the PSK interchange format (\fINVMeTLSkey\-1:<hmac>:<base64>:\fR), as created by \fInvme keys gen\-tls\-psk\fR\&.
.sp
nvme\-keysd puts the PSK in the keyring named by \fIkeyring\fR (default \fI\&.nvme\fR)\&. The key identity is built from the host NQN and the subsystem NQN, as \fInvme connect\fR does\&. A key that is already there is not written again\&. Any other key for the same host NQN and subsystem NQN is revoked, so that the kernel finds only one key for the pair\&.
.sp
Without a \fIkmip\fR entry, nvme\-keysd imports the keys and exits\&. With a \fIkmip\fR entry, it keeps running: see KMIP\&. nvme\-keysd never revokes a key because its entry was removed from the configuration\&.
.sp
The unit does not start when no entry of the fabrics configuration has a \fItls\-key\-source\fR other than \fIinline\fR\&. In that case, nvme\-keysd does not run, and the unit does not load the nvme_keyring module\&.
.SH "CREDENTIALS"
.sp
Each credential is a file in /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/, encrypted with systemd\-creds(1)\&. The directory is installed with mode 0700\&. The file name is the credential name\&. nvme\-keysd decrypts the file through systemd\-creds, which checks that the name in the credential is the file name\&. When \fI\-\-name\fR is not given, systemd\-creds takes the name from the output file name:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
# systemd\-creds encrypt psk\&.txt /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/vol1\-psk
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
and in nvme\-fabrics\&.conf:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[Subsystem]
nqn        = nqn\&.2024\-01\&.com\&.example:storage\&.vol1
controller = transport=tcp;traddr=192\&.0\&.2\&.10;trsvcid=4420
tls        = true
tls\-key\-source = systemd\-creds
tls\-key    = vol1\-psk
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
After adding or changing credentials, restart nvme\-keysd to import them:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
# systemctl restart nvme\-keysd
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
nvme\-keysd logs an error for a credential that it cannot decrypt, and imports the other keys\&.
.sp
nvme\-keysd needs systemd 257 or later\&.
.SH "KMIP"
.sp
With \fItls\-key\-source = kmip:<name>\fR, nvme\-keysd gets the PSK from a key management server (KMS) that supports KMIP 1\&.4\&. \fI<name>\fR is a \fI[kmip:<name>]\fR section of nvme\-keysd\&.conf\&. When only one KMS is configured, \fItls\-key\-source = kmip\fR is enough\&. \fItls\-key\fR is the Name of the key on the KMS\&.
.sp
The key on the KMS is one of:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Secret Data that holds the PSK in the PSK interchange format\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
A Symmetric Key of 32 bytes (SHA\-256) or 48 bytes (SHA\-384)\&.
.RE
.sp
Only a key in the Active state is used\&. Two Active keys with the same Name are an error\&.
.sp
nvme\-keysd itself has no network access\&. For privilege separation, a helper process with no privileges handles all communication with the KMS, over TLS\&. A client certificate is used when one is configured\&. The helper logs its errors in the journal, under the identifier nvme\-keysd\-kmip\&.
.sp
The servers of a KMS are nodes of one cluster, and hold the same keys\&. They are tried in order, until one answers\&. Different KMSes are asked in parallel\&.
.sp
nvme\-keysd does not delay the boot for a KMS\&. The services that start after nvme\-keysd find the keys from systemd credentials in the keyring, but a KMIP key can come later\&. nvme\-keysd keeps running and gets the KMIP keys in the background\&. When it cannot get a key, it tries again after 5 seconds, then after twice the previous delay, up to 5 minutes\&. A connection that needs a KMIP key can therefore start before the key is in the keyring\&. nvme\-discoverd connects again later\&. A single \fInvme connect\fR does not\&.
.sp
Setup example\&. The client private key is a systemd credential in /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/kmip/\&. systemd gives it to the helper as \fIkmip_<file>\fR, so \fI\-\-name\fR must be \fIkmip_\fR and the file name:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
# systemd\-creds encrypt \-\-name=kmip_my\-client\-key client\-key\&.pem \e
        /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/kmip/my\-client\-key
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
The CA and the client certificate are not secret\&. They must be readable by any user, because the helper runs without privileges\&. In nvme\-keysd\&.conf:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[kmip:my\-kms]
server      = kms1\&.example\&.com
server      = kms2\&.example\&.com
ca          = /usr/local/etc/nvme/kmip/ca\&.pem
client\-cert = /usr/local/etc/nvme/kmip/client\&.pem
client\-key  = my\-client\-key
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
and in nvme\-fabrics\&.conf:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[Subsystem]
nqn        = nqn\&.2024\-01\&.com\&.example:storage\&.vol1
controller = transport=tcp;traddr=192\&.0\&.2\&.10;trsvcid=4420
tls        = true
tls\-key\-source = kmip:my\-kms
tls\-key    = my\-vol1\-psk
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\-c <FILE>, \-\-config=<FILE>
.RS 4
Use <FILE> instead of /usr/local/etc/nvme/nvme\-keysd\&.conf\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-fabrics\-config=<FILE>
.RS 4
Read the fabrics configuration from <FILE> instead of /usr/local/etc/nvme/nvme\-fabrics\&.conf\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-creds\-dir=<DIR>
.RS 4
Read the encrypted credentials from <DIR> instead of /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-kmip\-test\-addr=<PATH>
.RS 4
Connect to the nvme\-keysd\-kmip Varlink socket at <PATH> instead of /run/nvme/keysd/kmip\&. For tests\&.
.RE
.PP
\-\-should\-start
.RS 4
Exit with status 0 if an entry of the fabrics configuration has a
\fItls\-key\-source\fR
other than
\fIinline\fR, and with status 1 otherwise\&. The unit runs this check before it starts nvme\-keysd\&.
.RE
.PP
\-d, \-\-debug
.RS 4
Enable debug\-level logging for both nvme\-keysd and the libnvme calls it makes, overriding the configured debug\-level\&.
.RE
.PP
\-h, \-\-help
.RS 4
Print usage and exit\&.
.RE
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
.sp
nvme\-keysd\&.conf holds the daemon\(cqs own settings, in a \fI[Global]\fR section, and the KMSes, in \fI[kmip:<name>]\fR sections:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
[Global]
debug\-level = info
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.sp
\fIdebug\-level\fR (err, warn, info, or debug; default info) sets the log threshold\&. \fI\-\-debug\fR on the command line overrides it\&.
.sp
Each \fI[kmip:<name>]\fR section describes a KMS:
.PP
\fIserver\fR
.RS 4
Host name or IP address\&. Required\&. Repeat it for each server of the cluster\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIport\fR
.RS 4
TCP port of the servers\&. Default 5696\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIca\fR
.RS 4
CA certificate that signed the server certificates (PEM)\&. Default: the system CAs\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIclient\-cert\fR
.RS 4
Client certificate (PEM)\&. Requires
\fIclient\-key\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fIclient\-key\fR
.RS 4
File name of the client private key in /usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/kmip/\&. Requires
\fIclient\-cert\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fItimeout\fR
.RS 4
Time limit for one server: connect, TLS handshake and requests\&. Default 10s\&. Each TCP connect fails after 3 seconds at most\&.
.RE
.sp
A missing file, or a missing key, keeps the default\&.
.SH "FILES"
.PP
/usr/local/etc/nvme/nvme\-keysd\&.conf
.RS 4
The daemon\(cqs own configuration; see CONFIGURATION\&.
.RE
.PP
/usr/local/etc/nvme/nvme\-keysd\&.conf\&.sample
.RS 4
A commented example of nvme\-keysd\&.conf with every setting at its default\&. It is not read; copy it to nvme\-keysd\&.conf to change a setting\&.
.RE
.PP
/usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/
.RS 4
The encrypted credentials; see CREDENTIALS\&.
.RE
.PP
/usr/local/etc/nvme/creds/kmip/
.RS 4
The encrypted client private keys for the KMSes; see KMIP\&.
.RE
